us":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Shape of X: (235795, 49) | Shape of y: (235795,)\n"]},{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 1000x750 with 1 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["# EXERCISE 3: Constructing X, y and Analyzing Target Correlations\n","cols_to_drop = ['FILENAME', 'URL', 'Domain', 'TLD', 'Title', 'label', 'IsPhishing', 'URLSimilarityIndex']\n","\n","### START CODE HERE ### (≈ 3 lines of code)\n","X = df.drop(columns=cols_to_drop)\n","y = df['IsPhishing']\n","correlations = X.corrwith(y).sort_values()\n","### END CODE HERE ###\n","\n","print(\"Shape of X:\", X.shape, \"| Shape of y:\", y.shape)\n","\n","# Plot Top 15 Negatively and Top 15 Positively Correlated Features\n","top_corr = pd.concat([correlations.head(15), correlations.tail(15)])\n","plt.figure(figsize=(10, 7.5))\n","colors = ['#16a34a' if c < 0 else '#dc2626' for c in top_corr.values]\n","top_corr.plot(kind='barh', color=colors)\n","plt.axvline(0, color='black', linewidth=0.8)\n","plt.title(\"Top 15 Negatively (Benign) and Positively (Phishing) Correlated Features with IsPhishing\")\n","plt.xlabel(\"Pearson Correlation Coefficient (r)\")\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"0e61777f","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.005774,"end_time":"2026-11-09T18:11:57.262387+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-09T18:11:57.256613+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["---\n","### 💡 Part 1 Reflection Questions\n","\n","1. **Looking at your plot for `IsHTTPS`, what percentage of phishing sites currently use an HTTPS certificate? Why is a static rule *\"If HTTPS then Legitimate\"* dangerous?**\n","   * **Answer:** According to the `binary_stats` table and bar chart, **[[XX.X]] %** of phishing URLs use HTTPS (vs. **[[YY.Y]] %** of legitimate ones). The rule \"HTTPS ⇒ Legitimate\" is dangerous because an HTTPS certificate only proves that the channel is encrypted and that the owner controls the domain; it says nothing about the owner's intent. Free, automated certificate authorities (e.g., Let's Encrypt) let attackers obtain valid certificates in seconds, so a large share of phishing sites would pass the rule (high False Negatives) and users would get a false sense of security. A static rule is also trivial for an adversary to adapt to.\n","\n","2. **Why do phishing websites typically have very few internal links (`NoOfSelfRef`) but very frequently contain a password field (`HasPasswordField`)?**\n","   * **Answer:** A phishing page is a disposable, single-purpose page that clones a login form: it has no real site structure, so it contains few links pointing to its own domain (its links are empty, or point to the impersonated brand). Its only goal is credential harvesting, so the password field is the core of the attack. Legitimate sites have rich internal navigation (many self-references) and only some of their pages contain a password field. The combination \"few internal links + password field\" is therefore highly discriminative.\n","\n","3. **What would happen in production if we left the `FILENAME`, `URL`, or `Domain` column in our training matrix?**\n","   * **Answer:** These are high-cardinality, nearly unique strings. The model would memorize specific URLs/domains (overfitting) instead of learning general lexical and HTML behavior, giving optimistic test metrics but failing on new URLs. Attackers register new domains constantly, so the model would not generalize to unseen domains and would behave like a static blocklist. These columns can also leak the label (e.g., the same domains appearing in train and test), inflating performance artificially.\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":7,"id":"b639d6f3","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-09T18:11:57.275915Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-09T18:11:57.275377Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-09T18:11:57.698065Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-09T18:11:57.696632Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.431761,"end_time":"2026-11-09T18:11:57.70014+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-09T18:11:57.268379+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Train shape: (176846, 49) | Test shape: (58949, 49)\n","Mean of X_train_scaled (URLLength): -0.0\n","Std of X_train_scaled (URLLength): 1.0\n"]}],"source":["# EXERCISE 4: Stratified Split and Leakage-Free Standardization\n","\n","### START CODE HERE ### (≈ 4 lines of code)\n","X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.25, stratify=y, random_state=SEED)\n","scaler = StandardScaler()\n","X_train_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X_train)\n","X_test_scaled = scaler.transform(X_test)\n","### END CODE HERE ###\n","\n","# Retain column names and indices for weight and SHAP interpretability\n","X_train_scaled = pd.DataFrame(X_train_scaled, columns=X.columns, index=X_train.index)\n","X_test_scaled  = pd.DataFrame(X_test_scaled, columns=X.columns, index=X_test.index)\n","\n","print(\"Train shape:\", X_train_scaled.shape, \"| Test shape:\", X_test_scaled.shape)\n","print(\"Mean of X_train_scaled (URLLength):\", round(X_train_scaled['URLLength'].mean(), 4))\n","print(\"Std of X_train_scaled (URLLength):\", round(X_train_scaled['URLLength'].std(), 4))"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"50c17ad6","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.005588,"end_time":"2026-11-09T18:11:57.711328+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-09T18:11:57.70574+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["---\n","### 💡 Part 2 Reflection Questions\n","\n","1. **Compare the scale of `LineOfCode` (values up to 10,000+) and `IsHTTPS` (0 or 1) before standardization. Why would L1/L2 regularization be skewed without `StandardScaler`?**\n","   * **Answer:** `LineOfCode` ranges over thousands while `IsHTTPS` is only 0 or 1. The L1/L2 penalty ($\\lambda\\|w\\|_1$ or $\\lambda\\|w\\|_2^2$) is applied to the coefficients regardless of the feature units. A large-scale feature only needs a tiny coefficient to have an effect, so it is barely penalized, while a small-scale binary feature needs a large coefficient and is heavily shrunk (or set to 0 by L1). Feature selection and shrinkage would then depend on the measurement units rather than on how informative the feature is. Standardization (mean 0, std 1) puts all coefficients on a comparable scale and also helps the solver converge.\n","\n","2. **Precisely explain why calling `scaler.fit_transform(X)` before `train_test_split` constitutes Preprocessing Data Leakage.**\n","   * **Answer:** `fit_transform(X)` computes the mean $\\mu$ and standard deviation $\\sigma$ using all rows, including the future test rows. The training data are then transformed with parameters that contain information from the test set, so the test set is no longer truly \"unseen\" and the evaluation becomes optimistic. In production, the statistics of future URLs are unknown at training time. The correct procedure is to split first, `fit` the scaler on `X_train` only, then apply `transform` to `X_test` with $\\mu_{train}$ and $\\sigma_{train}$.\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":8,"id":"a6bc7b8e","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-09T18:11:57.727168Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-09T18:11:57.726273Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-09T18:12:02.613244Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-09T18:12:02.612338Z"},"papermill":{"duration":4.90015,"end_time":"2026-11-09T18:12:02.61733+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-09T18:11:57.71718+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"data":{"text/html":["<div>\n","<style scoped>\n","    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n","        vertical-align: middle;\n","    }\n","\n","    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n","        vertical-align: top;\n","    }\n","\n","    .dataframe thead th {\n","        text-align: right;\n","    }\n","</style>\n","<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n",